WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.495
SPEAKER 1: We've talked a bit
about how Kubeflow can help

00:00:02.495 --> 00:00:03.810
and seen one way to set it up.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.560
There's a lot you can
do once it's set up.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.852
So let's break down some of
the components of Kubeflow.

00:00:07.852 --> 00:00:11.118
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.770
Today we'll go through
and give an overview

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.893
of some of the biggest
components of Kubeflow.

00:00:15.893 --> 00:00:18.060
If you want to check out
the documentation directly,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.602
check out the link
below to read more

00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:21.090
about the components
of Kubeflow.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.130
As you've heard us say
before, machine learning

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.380
can be complicated.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.130
There's lots of different
pieces involved,

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.460
like handling and validating
your data, training your model,

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.040
evaluating it, and
eventually serving it.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.280
Since Kubeflow handles multiple
parts of the ML experience,

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.800
it's made to be
flexible and composable.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.520
You can use the
pieces you want to

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.935
and swap out for the
other parts as needed.

00:00:41.935 --> 00:00:44.310
Let's take a quick look at
some of the most commonly used

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.960
components available to you.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.090
We'll do more deep
dives in future videos.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.365
First and foremost,
there's the user interface.

00:00:50.365 --> 00:00:51.990
You can think of this
as your dashboard

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.700
to manage your ML projects.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.190
You can launch other components,
get useful information

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.670
about your cluster, and find
quick links to documentation.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.312
If you're working
with multiple teams,

00:01:01.312 --> 00:01:03.270
you may want to set up
namespaces for each team

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:04.379
to work in.

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.540
Users who have access
to multiple namespaces

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.160
can choose which one they're
working on at the top.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.540
There's a lot more behind
the interface, though.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:14.010
Kubeflow also comes with an
integrated Jupyter notebook

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:15.180
environment.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.310
Notebooks are useful
for doing quick tests,

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.980
developing models, and
writing application code.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.980
It's not uncommon for ML
engineers and data scientists

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.320
to run their notebooks
locally and get constrained

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.300
by resources, like
trying to train

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.640
a large model on your laptop.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.830
Kubeflow also supports
multi-user support

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.660
through namespaces,
so it's easier

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.307
to collaborate
and manage access.

00:01:36.307 --> 00:01:37.890
Having notebooks in
your cluster makes

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.600
it easy to run jobs
where resources

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.730
can be dynamically scaled.

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.370
We'll talk more about
notebooks in the next video.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.530
Another important
component is Katib,

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.530
which can be used for automated
hyperparameter tuning.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.630
Hyperparameter tuning
plays a major part

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.790
in developing good
models, and manually

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:57.110
searching a hyperparameter
space can take a lot of effort.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.580
Katib helps you optimize
your hyperparameter values

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.160
around a metric you've
chosen, such as the accuracy

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:03.340
of the model.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.160
It's a helpful way
to find and visualize

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.170
the optimum
configuration to prepare

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.490
your model for production.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:11.370
Next up, Kubeflow Pipelines
is a powerful platform

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.795
to help you build
end-to-end ML workflows.

00:02:13.795 --> 00:02:15.420
Pipelines lets you
build a set of steps

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.730
to handle everything
from collecting data

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.240
to serving your model.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.030
It's based on
containers, so each step

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.050
is portable and scalable.

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:25.680
You can run your same Pipelines
code across environments

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.070
and share it with others.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:28.920
And since each step
is independent,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.560
you can reuse Pipelines
steps for different pipelines

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.850
and experiments.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.280
Chances are you're
probably going

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.950
to want to train your model.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.490
Kubeflow supports several
ML frameworks for training,

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.820
such as TensorFlow and PyTorch.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.260
Kubeflow defines custom
Kubernetes resources

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.270
for distributed
training jobs, so you

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.160
can create a config
file that specifies

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.320
the details you want
to use for training

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.580
and let it handle the rest.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.070
You have the
flexibility to choose

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.590
the tools you want to
use, or you're already

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.080
using, to train and
retrain your model.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.460
We'll walk through some
training in future videos.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.525
Of course, your models
may not be too useful

00:03:02.525 --> 00:03:04.650
unless you can actually
make predictions with them.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.110
Kubeflow can serve your models
with multiple frameworks

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.590
like TF Serving,
KFServing, and Seldon Core.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.400
You could even plug in your
own serving system if needed,

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.950
but KFServing can help abstract
most common ML frameworks.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.260
It's built on Knative,
a framework for running

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.300
serverless apps on Kubernetes.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.460
That way, you can serve
your model and access

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.040
all the resources you need
without having to handle

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.480
the complexities directly.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.910
This is just a brief overview
of the major components

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:30.720
in Kubeflow.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.270
There's lots of examples you
can try out for yourself.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.950
But we'll also go
deeper and talk more

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.983
about each of these
in upcoming videos.

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:39.150
Check out the link below
to try it out for yourself,

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:39.900
and stay tuned.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

